Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• player.player_id: unique identifier to link every delivery back to the batter who faced it.  
• player.player_name: to show human‑readable names while validating intermediate results.  
• player.country_name: required to aggregate every player’s batting figures by country.  
• ball_by_ball.match_id / over_id / ball_id: join keys that let us line‑up every delivery with the runs that were scored off it.  
• ball_by_ball.striker: the player_id of the batter who actually faced the ball (the only one who can score the runs in batsman_scored).  
• batsman_scored.match_id / over_id / ball_id: join keys that hold the number of runs scored off the very same ball.  
• batsman_scored.runs_scored: runs to be summed for every player in every match.  
• match.match_id / season_id (optional): allow checks across specific seasons, but here we keep “all seasons”, so they are mostly helpful for sanity checks or filtering in ad‑hoc tests.  

Typical conditions you may see  
• Joining ball_by_ball and batsman_scored on (match_id, over_id, ball_id).  
• Filtering a subset of players or matches with WHERE player.country_name = 'India' (etc.) when validating.  
• GROUP BY player_id, match_id to total a player’s runs in one match.  
• GROUP BY player_id to derive each player’s average runs per match.  
• GROUP BY country_name to obtain the country‑level average of those player‑level averages.  

Below are 10 exploratory queries (each different) that move step‑by‑step from very simple inspections to a complete answer.  
Every query is limited to 20 rows (or 5 for the final TOP‑N) and uses DISTINCT wherever it makes sense.  

```sql
-- 1) List all countries that have at least one player in the database
SELECT DISTINCT "country_name"
FROM "player"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Show a handful of Indian players so we know the IDs we might test with
SELECT DISTINCT "player_id", "player_name"
FROM "player"
WHERE "country_name" = 'India'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Take a quick look at the raw scoring table
SELECT DISTINCT "match_id", "over_id", "ball_id", "runs_scored"
FROM "batsman_scored"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Marry every delivery to the batter who faced it and the runs he scored
SELECT b."match_id",
       b."over_id",
       b."ball_id",
       b."striker"        AS "player_id",
       bs."runs_scored"
FROM   "ball_by_ball"  AS b
JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS bs
  ON   b."match_id" = bs."match_id"
 AND   b."over_id"  = bs."over_id"
 AND   b."ball_id"  = bs."ball_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Total runs made by each player in one match (choose the first 20 matches seen)
SELECT t."match_id",
       t."player_id",
       SUM(t."runs_scored") AS "runs_in_match"
FROM (
        SELECT b."match_id",
               b."striker"        AS "player_id",
               bs."runs_scored"
        FROM   "ball_by_ball"  AS b
        JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS bs
          ON   b."match_id" = bs."match_id"
         AND   b."over_id"  = bs."over_id"
         AND   b."ball_id"  = bs."ball_id"
     ) AS t
GROUP BY t."match_id", t."player_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Count how many matches each player has batted in
SELECT t."player_id",
       COUNT(DISTINCT t."match_id") AS "matches_played"
FROM (
        SELECT b."match_id",
               b."striker" AS "player_id"
        FROM   "ball_by_ball" AS b
     ) AS t
GROUP BY t."player_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Compute every player’s average runs per match
WITH match_runs AS (
    SELECT b."match_id",
           b."striker" AS "player_id",
           SUM(bs."runs_scored") AS "runs_in_match"
    FROM   "ball_by_ball"  AS b
    JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS bs
      ON   b."match_id" = bs."match_id"
     AND   b."over_id"  = bs."over_id"
     AND   b."ball_id"  = bs."ball_id"
    GROUP BY b."match_id", b."striker"
)
SELECT mr."player_id",
       AVG(mr."runs_in_match") AS "avg_runs_per_match"
FROM   match_runs AS mr
GROUP BY mr."player_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Attach player country to the player‑level averages
WITH match_runs AS (
    SELECT b."match_id",
           b."striker" AS "player_id",
           SUM(bs."runs_scored") AS "runs_in_match"
    FROM   "ball_by_ball"  AS b
    JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS bs
      ON   b."match_id" = bs."match_id"
     AND   b."over_id"  = bs."over_id"
     AND   b."ball_id"  = bs."ball_id"
    GROUP BY b."match_id", b."striker"
),
player_avg AS (
    SELECT mr."player_id",
           AVG(mr."runs_in_match") AS "avg_runs_per_match"
    FROM   match_runs AS mr
    GROUP BY mr."player_id"
)
SELECT pa."player_id",
       p."player_name",
       p."country_name",
       pa."avg_runs_per_match"
FROM   player_avg AS pa
JOIN   "player" AS p
  ON   pa."player_id" = p."player_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Country‑level average of those player averages
WITH match_runs AS (
    SELECT b."match_id",
           b."striker" AS "player_id",
           SUM(bs."runs_scored") AS "runs_in_match"
    FROM   "ball_by_ball"  AS b
    JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS bs
      ON   b."match_id" = bs."match_id"
     AND   b."over_id"  = bs."over_id"
     AND   b."ball_id"  = bs."ball_id"
    GROUP BY b."match_id", b."striker"
),
player_avg AS (
    SELECT mr."player_id",
           AVG(mr."runs_in_match") AS "avg_runs_per_match"
    FROM   match_runs AS mr
    GROUP BY mr."player_id"
)
SELECT p."country_name",
       AVG(pa."avg_runs_per_match") AS "country_batting_avg"
FROM   player_avg AS pa
JOIN   "player" AS p
  ON   pa."player_id" = p."player_id"
GROUP BY p."country_name"
ORDER BY "country_batting_avg" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) FINAL: Top‑5 countries with the highest average of their players' average runs per match
WITH match_runs AS (
    SELECT b."match_id",
           b."striker" AS "player_id",
           SUM(bs."runs_scored") AS "runs_in_match"
    FROM   "ball_by_ball"  AS b
    JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS bs
      ON   b."match_id" = bs."match_id"
     AND   b."over_id"  = bs."over_id"
     AND   b."ball_id"  = bs."ball_id"
    GROUP BY b."match_id", b."striker"
),
player_avg AS (
    SELECT mr."player_id",
           AVG(mr."runs_in_match") AS "avg_runs_per_match"
    FROM   match_runs AS mr
    GROUP BY mr."player_id"
),
country_avg AS (
    SELECT p."country_name",
           AVG(pa."avg_runs_per_match") AS "country_batting_avg"
    FROM   player_avg AS pa
    JOIN   "player" AS p
      ON   pa."player_id" = p."player_id"
    GROUP BY p."country_name"
)
SELECT "country_name",
       "country_batting_avg"
FROM   country_avg
ORDER BY "country_batting_avg" DESC
LIMIT 5;
```

